================================================================
* SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS
================================================================
Conceptual ZTA components for 6G deployment (as discussed in paper):
  - Identity Provider (IdP): manages user/device identities, credentials,
    and trust anchors across heterogeneous 6G domains.
  - Policy Engine (PE): AI-assisted decision engine evaluating identity,
    device posture, behavioral context, threat intelligence, and risk
    score in real time.
  - Policy Administrator (PA): generates session tokens, manages
    authentication flows, enforces just-in-time privilege elevation.
  - Policy Enforcement Point (PEP): gateway/enforcer at every network
    segment (RAN, core, edge, cloud, application) — can be SW or HW.
  - Continuous Monitoring System: telemetry collection across network
    traffic, user behavior, device health, application logs; feeds ML
    anomaly detection.
  - Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) module: supports OTP, biometric,
    PKI, hardware tokens — adaptable to device capabilities.
  - Risk Assessment Engine: dynamic trust scoring based on device
    posture, location, time, behavior patterns, threat intel feeds.
  - AI-assisted Security Analytics: ML models for threat detection,
    behavioral profiling, automated policy adjustment.
  - Secure 6G Core Network: 3GPP-compliant core with ZT integration
    at AMF, SMF, UPF, NEF, etc.
  - Edge/Cloud Infrastructure: distributed PEPs and monitoring agents
    at edge nodes and cloud data centers.
  - Quantum-safe cryptography modules: post-quantum cryptographic
    algorithms for long-term security of keys and communications.

================================================================
* RESULTS
================================================================
Findings from the survey and case-study analysis:

1. ZTA effectiveness for 6G:
   - Significantly improves security posture by eliminating implicit trust
     and enforcing continuous verification at every network layer.
   - Reduces lateral movement: microsegmentation isolates breaches and
     prevents attacker propagation.
   - Protects distributed resources: PEP at every segment (RAN, edge,
     core, cloud) ensures consistent policy regardless of device location.
   - Supports heterogeneous environments: identity-centric approach works
     across IoT, smartphones, vehicles, UAVs, industrial controllers.

2. Authentication technique comparison findings:
   - MFA provides strong baseline security but adds latency and usability
     friction; behavioral/continuous authentication offers highest security
     but requires significant compute resources and ML infrastructure.
   - No single authentication method fits all 6G use cases — adaptive,
     context-aware multi-factor auth is recommended.
   - Lightweight continuous auth schemes are needed for resource-
     constrained IoT devices.

3. Access control model findings:
   - ABAC offers the best balance of granularity and scalability for 6G
     ZTA, supporting dynamic policy based on subject/object/action/
     environment attributes.
   - PBAC provides the most fine-grained control but introduces policy
     management complexity.

4. Case-study insights:
   - Smart healthcare: ZTA successfully prevents unauthorized access to
     patient data and medical IoT devices; continuous monitoring detects
     anomalous behavior in real time.
   - Industrial IoT: microsegmentation limits blast radius from compromised
     OT devices; ZT policy engine adapts to device posture changes.
   - Autonomous vehicles/V2X: ZTA secures communication between vehicles,
     infrastructure, and cloud; identity-based auth for vehicle-to-everything
     prevents spoofing and replay attacks.
   - Smart cities: distributed PEPs at edge nodes enforce consistent
     policies across thousands of heterogeneous sensors and actuators.

5. Remaining challenges identified:
   - Deployment complexity: integrating ZTA with legacy infrastructure and
     existing 5G/6G core network functions is non-trivial.
   - Scalability: continuous monitoring and real-time policy evaluation
     for billions of devices poses computation and bandwidth challenges.
   - Latency overhead: authentication and policy checks add delay —
     critical for URLLC applications requiring sub-millisecond response.
   - Privacy: continuous behavioral monitoring raises data privacy
     concerns; privacy-preserving ZTA mechanisms needed.
   - Interoperability: ZTA components from different vendors must
     interoperate across heterogeneous 6G multi-domain environments.
   - Resource constraints: lightweight ZT implementations needed for
     IoT sensors, wearables, and other low-power devices.
   - Quantum threats: current cryptographic foundations of ZTA (PKI,
     TLS) vulnerable to quantum attacks; post-quantum transition needed.

